      en: "Mushenge, also known as Nsheng, is the traditional capital of the Kuba Kingdom and a center of extraordinary cultural and artistic achievement in the Democratic Republic of the Congo. The kingdom was established in the 17th century and developed a highly sophisticated court etiquette along with a unique social structure. Mushenge serves as the residence of the Nyim, the King of the Kuba, and houses the impressive royal palace built using traditional techniques and organic materials. The city is world-renowned for its artistic output, particularly the Kasai velvets (intricate raffia palm textiles) as well as ornate woodcarvings and ceremonial masks. The aesthetic sophistication of Kuba art significantly influenced Western artists, including Henri Matisse. Beyond its art, Mushenge is a living site of tradition where historical rites and the oral history of the royal lineage are meticulously preserved. The architecture of the palace complex reflects the cosmological beliefs of the people. As the cultural heart of the Kasai region, Mushenge stands as a testament to the enduring legacy of African monarchies.",
